// Practice quiz: Abstract classes
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, and code is described in words where GIFT syntax would clash.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/inheritance-and-design/abstract-classes

::o1 mixed methods::Which members may an abstract class contain? {
~Only abstract methods#Not quite. It may also have concrete methods with bodies.
~Only constants and abstract methods#Not quite. It can have ordinary instance fields too.
~No constructors#Not quite. It may declare constructors, which subclasses call.
=Abstract methods, concrete methods, fields and constructors#Correct. An abstract class is a full class that simply cannot be instantiated.
}

::o1 abstract method rule::A class declares an abstract method but the class itself is not marked abstract. What happens? {
=It does not compile#Correct. Any class with an abstract method must itself be declared abstract.
~The method gets an empty body#Not quite. Java never supplies a body for an abstract method.
~It compiles, and calling the method throws at run time#Not quite. The compiler rejects it before it runs.
~The class becomes abstract automatically#Not quite. You must write abstract on the class yourself.
}

::o2 no new::Why does new Shape() fail when Shape is abstract? {
~Abstract classes have no constructors#Not quite. They can have constructors; subclasses call them.
~Shape must be an interface#Not quite. Abstract classes are a separate kind of type.
~It works if Shape has no abstract methods#Not quite. Marking a class abstract forbids new even without abstract methods.
=An abstract class may have methods with no body, so Java will not create a bare instance#Correct. You create a concrete subclass object instead and can refer to it as a Shape.
}

::o2 subclass duty::A concrete subclass of an abstract class must do what? {
=Provide a body for every inherited abstract method#Correct. Otherwise the subclass must be declared abstract too.
~Override every concrete method#Not quite. Concrete methods can be inherited as they are.
~Redeclare all inherited fields#Not quite. Fields are inherited without redeclaring them.
~Call every superclass method in its constructor#Not quite. There is no such requirement.
}

::o3 shared state::Three report types share a title field and a common header method, and only their body() differs. Which design fits best? {
~An interface with a default method holding the title#Not quite. Interfaces cannot hold per-object instance fields.
=An abstract Report class with the field, the header method and an abstract body()#Correct. An abstract class holds shared state and code, and leaves the varying step to subclasses.
~Copy the field and header into each class#Not quite. That duplicates code that the subclasses share.
~One class with a switch on report type#Not quite. Each new type would mean editing the switch.
}

::o3 when not::Two unrelated classes only need the same single method signature and share no state. Which fits better? {
~An abstract class with one abstract method#Not quite. It works, but it uses up each class's single superclass for nothing shared.
~A concrete superclass with an empty method#Not quite. Callers could create the meaningless superclass directly.
=An interface#Correct. With nothing to share but a contract, an interface avoids forcing a superclass on them.
~A static utility class#Not quite. That gives no common type to program against.
}
